::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: fraction = decimal = percent; percent means out of 100; unit rate means per 1 unit.
What is 3/4 written as a decimal?
{
~A. 0.34#This confuses the numerator and denominator with decimal digits. Divide 3 by 4 to convert the fraction.
=B. 0.75#Correct. Since 3 divided by 4 equals 0.75, 3/4 = 0.75.
~C. 3.4#This places the numerator and denominator beside each other instead of dividing. A fraction represents division.
~D. 75#This gives the percent value without the percent symbol. As a decimal, the value is 0.75.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: fraction = decimal = percent; percent means out of 100; unit rate means per 1 unit.
What is 0.6 written as a percent?
{
~A. 6%#This moves the decimal only one place. To write a decimal as a percent, multiply by 100.
~B. 0.6%#This keeps the decimal unchanged. Percent means out of 100, so the value must be multiplied by 100.
~C. 600%#This moves the decimal one place too far. 0.6 is less than 1, so it is 60%, not 600%.
=D. 60%#Correct. 0.6 times 100 = 60, so 0.6 = 60%.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: fraction = decimal = percent; percent means out of 100; unit rate means per 1 unit.
What is 35% written as a decimal?
{
=A. 0.35#Correct. Percent means out of 100, so 35% = 35/100 = 0.35.
~B. 3.5#This moves the decimal one place instead of two. Divide the percent by 100 to convert to a decimal.
~C. 35#This keeps the percent number unchanged. A percent must be divided by 100 to become a decimal.
~D. 0.035#This moves the decimal three places. For percent to decimal, move two places left.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: fraction = decimal = percent; percent means out of 100; unit rate means per 1 unit.
What is 25% of 48?
{
~A. 24#This treats 25% like 50%. Remember that 25% is one-fourth of the whole.
~B. 25#This uses the percent number as the answer instead of finding the percent of 48.
=C. 12#Correct. 25% = 1/4, and one-fourth of 48 is 12.
~D. 120#This multiplies by 2.5 instead of 0.25. Convert 25% to 0.25 before multiplying.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Visual reference: fraction = decimal = percent; percent means out of 100; unit rate means per 1 unit.
A student reads 72 pages in 4 hours. What is the unit rate in pages per hour?
{
~A. 76 pages per hour#This adds the numbers instead of dividing. A unit rate compares to 1 hour.
=B. 18 pages per hour#Correct. 72 ÃƒÂ· 4 = 18, so the student reads 18 pages per hour.
~C. 68 pages per hour#This subtracts the numbers instead of dividing. Use pages divided by hours.
~D. 288 pages per hour#This multiplies the numbers. A unit rate requires division by the number of hours.
}
